  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1140 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nDry and warm conditions will continue through the remainder of\nthe week as an upper ridge sets up over the region. Expect daily\nhighs temperatures in the 70s and low 80s with lows in the 40s\nand 50s. Afternoon minimum relative humidity values, which have\nbeen the main story this week, will remain below 30% areawide\nboth Thursday and Friday afternoon. Without meaningful rainfall\nin the past month or so and while in the middle of a transitional\nperiod that is traditionally dry, vegetation is forecast to be\nvery dry. Both the low atmospheric humidity and dry fuels will\nmean a continuation of the elevated fire danger that has plagued\nthe region this week.\n\nBy this weekend, the upper ridge axis will have shifted east,\nextending north and south along the Mississippi River Valley as\nan omega block. Normally this would lead to an increase in\ntemperatures, but NBM data from today points to a different\nstory: a cooling trend. What appears to be the case is that upper\nmoisture from the trough on the western periphery of the blocking\npattern will advect into the region. Most of this moisture will\nremain at 300 mb and above, leading to an expansive area of thick\ncirrus clouds, which will then lead to a decrease in solar\nheating. Therefore, highs will drop into the middle to low 70s. A\nside effect of the lower amount of insolation will be a shallower\nmixed boundary layer. With lower surface temperatures and less\nmixing of mid-level dry air to the surface, relative humidity\nvalues could jump back above 30% for the first time in about a\nweek for many areas.\n\nInto next week we will see a pattern change emerge as the omega\nblock breaks down. Zonal westerlies will then begin to take over\naloft. Ensembles have begun to converge on this solution, but are\nstill struggling to resolve the position and amplitude of\nshortwaves within the westerly flow. This has still led to a\ndecent amount of forecast uncertainty this forecast cycle,\nespecially regarding temperature forecasts with a potential cold\nfront by the middle of next week. Additionally, precipitation\nchances are still bleak as mid-level humidity aloft gets advected\nnorth of the region well before the zonal westerlies reach the\nregion, mitigating the potential for a front to produce\nprecipitation later in the week. Therefore, the remainder of the\nforecast beyond this weekend looks to be leaning towards more of\nthe same.",
